  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining cool and dry through Wednesday.\n\nNo changes tonight as calm conditions prevail. Light \nsoutheasterly gradients will favor an uptick in overnight \ntemperatures as high pressure drifts offshore. While still \ncooler than recent days, most can anticipate lows in the mid 50s\nto low 60s (mildest along the I-95 urban areas as well as near \nthe tidal Potomac River and Chesapeake Bay shorelines).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warmer with shower and thunderstorm chances \nincreasing for the second half of the week.\n\nBehind an exiting longwave trough that pushes into the Canadian\nMaritimes, mid/upper heights begin to build in earnest on \nWednesday. An expansive near 600-dm mid-level ridge initially \nover the southern U.S. will flex northward in time to a position \nover the TN Valley by late Wednesday. These above average \nheights remain a fixture in the forecast through Thursday. This \nsupports a return of warmer temperatures along with building \nhumidity levels. Daily highs push back into the mid/upper 80s, \nlocally reaching the low 90s in the warm spots. The return of \nthe humidity also favors milder nights with widespread lows in \nthe 60s.\n\nAny shower and thunderstorm chances will be minimal for much of\nthe area through Wednesday. Thereafter, warm/moisture advection\ncoupled with terrain circulations and daytime heating could \nresult in a pop up shower as early as late Wednesday afternoon \nnear and west of the Appalachian crest. Low precipitation probs\nexpand slowly eastward in this WAA pattern Wednesday night into\nThursday morning, with some of the latest hi-res guidance even\nbringing some activity just east of the Blue Ridge by daybreak\nThursday morning. Additional shower and thunderstorm chances\nenter the picture Thursday afternoon and evening focused west of\nI-95. With modest instability and generally weak shear, the\nprospect for severe weather seems low, but it will be\nunseasonably hot so some gusty storms can't be ruled out.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled but uncertain pattern this weekend\ninto early next week.\n\nA high latitude trough digging across southeastern Canada \nshould be enough to nudge the upper ridge back toward the south-\ncentral U.S. by this weekend. This allows a cold front to push \nthrough early Friday which again brings a gradual cool down with\ndecreasing humidity into Saturday. However, this reprieve is \nsomewhat brief and uncertain as additional disturbances in the \natmosphere track toward the area late in the weekend, causing\nthe front to waver back and forth near the region. The overall \njet pattern turns a bit more complex as an array of features \nenter the picture. A broad upper low retrogrades across the \nGulf. At the same time, a myriad of shortwaves will eject across\nthe central U.S. toward the Mississippi Valley. How these \nimpulses behave along with where surface features reside will \ndictate any local weather impacts. However, all signs point \ntoward a more active pattern by Sunday into early next week.",
